
# Delete @MEMBER_CODE@'s SIGN key -- reversal of MEMBER_SIGN_KEY.hurl.tmpl
#. CONFIRMED LIVE (apps/join-api/tests/fixtures/xroad/
# unjoin.sign_key_delete.json, docs/decisions/xroad-770-notes.md #11 "What happens to
# a hosted member's SIGN key"): a hosted member's client-delete does NOT
# touch its SIGN key or certificate -- both survive completely intact on
# the host's token, status REGISTERED, active, OCSP good
# (unjoin.token_after_client_delete.json, taken AFTER the client stopped
# listing). This is therefore a REQUIRED, explicit step, not cleanup of an
# already-gone resource. One DELETE /keys/{key_id} returns 204 and removes
# the key AND its still-REGISTERED certificate together -- no separate
# certificate delete, disable or unregister first.
#
# {{@CAP_P@_sign_key_id}}: forward-provided by MEMBER_SIGN_KEY.hurl.tmpl,
# but the id used here should be RE-DERIVED by correlating
# keys[].certificates[].owner_id (PROBE_SS_SIGN_KEY.hurl.tmpl's own
# correlation, reused rather than re-built -- see hurl/steps.py's comment
# on ss.sign_key_csr), never trusted blind from a resumed job's context: a
# hosting server carries one identically-labelled "Sign key" per hosted
# member (security_server.hosted_on; four, live, under the since-retired
# lite profile at the time -- PROBE_SS_SIGN_KEY.hurl.tmpl's own comment),
# so a label match, or a captured id that predates a re-issued key, deletes
# a DIFFERENT agency's key.
DELETE https://{{@HOSTVAR@}}:4000/api/v1/keys/{{@CAP_P@_sign_key_id}}
X-XSRF-TOKEN: {{@SESS_P@_xsrf_token}}

HTTP 204
